<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The privacy manifest. Required since May 2024, and its absence is not a warning: the
  upload is rejected at intake with ITMS-91053 ("Missing API declaration") before any
  human sees the build. It is easy to miss precisely because nothing local catches it —
  the app builds, runs and tests clean without it.

  It lives in `streetw/` rather than beside the .xcodeproj, which is the opposite of where
  `streetw-Info.plist` sits and for the same reason. That directory is a
  PBXFileSystemSynchronizedRootGroup, so anything dropped in it is copied into the bundle
  as a resource — which is wrong for an Info.plist and exactly right for this, since Apple
  looks for it at the root of the built .app.

  Two things are declared and the distinction matters:

  - `NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes` is the part that blocks the upload. It is about *calling*
    an API Apple considers fingerprintable, regardless of what you do with the result.
  - `NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes` is the nutrition label, and it must agree with the
    answers given in App Store Connect. ASC is the authoritative copy; a contradiction
    between the two is its own review problem.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  No tracking, in Apple's specific sense of the word: nothing here is joined with data
	  from other companies' apps or sites, and nothing is sold to a data broker. This is
	  what keeps the app out of App Tracking Transparency — with this false, no
	  `NSUserTrackingUsageDescription` is needed and the ATT prompt must never appear.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>

	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<!--
			  The only required-reason API this app touches. It is used in six files —
			  `ServerSettings` (the server address and device token), `SizeProfileStore`,
			  `StyleStatementStore`, `RemoteSync`'s feed cursor, and the `@AppStorage`
			  flags in `ContentView`/`streetwApp`.

			  `CA92.1` is the right reason: every read and write is this app's own data,
			  in its own container. It is *not* `1C8F.1`, which covers a suite shared
			  through an App Group — the app group here carries the share-extension inbox
			  as files on disk, and no `UserDefaults(suiteName:)` exists anywhere in the
			  project.

			  Deliberately nothing else is declared. FileTimestamp in particular does not
			  apply: `SharedInbox.pending()` lists the inbox directory with
			  `includingPropertiesForKeys: nil` and orders by a date carried *inside* each
			  JSON payload, so no filesystem timestamp is ever read. Declaring a category
			  that isn't used is not a safe default — it puts a claim on the nutrition
			  label that the code cannot support.
			-->
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>

	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<!--
			  The device row: a server-issued bearer token and an APNs device token. No email
			  and no name of the user anywhere in this system — but the row is persistent and
			  everything below hangs off it, so it is reported as linked rather than
			  pretending pseudonymity is anonymity.
			-->
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeDeviceID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!--
			  Which brands are followed and which products are watched. This is the whole
			  point of the server: it polls once for everyone and sends a push when
			  something a follower cares about moves.

			  And the anonymous usage counts (`UsageCounts`, `POST /v1/usage`): how many
			  times a day the app was opened, each tab shown, a search run, a save or a fit
			  made, a push tapped. Analytics, so it is declared as a purpose here. The
			  counts themselves are added to shared totals on arrival and stored against
			  no device — but Apple's label answers "linked" per data type rather than per
			  purpose, and this type is already linked for the follows above, so it stays
			  linked rather than splitting the type in two. App Store Connect must say the
			  same: Product Interaction, App Functionality and Analytics.
			-->
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeProductInteraction</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!--
			  The size profile and the gender filter, sent so the server can decide whether
			  a restock is worth waking someone for; the country a person shops in, so the
			  feed comes from that country's shop (a setting defaulting to the phone's region,
			  never a position — which is why this is not Coarse Location); and each profile's
			  alerts switch.

			  Note what is *not* here, and is not an oversight: saves, boards, fits, the
			  style statement, the taste vector and every dismissal stay on the device by
			  design. The recommender ships candidate vectors down and compares locally
			  precisely so none of that has to be collected.
			-->
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherDataTypes</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!--
			  Sign in with Apple's stable identifier (`accounts.apple_subject`), optional, and
			  what an account is recognised by on a new phone. Apple mints a different one per
			  team, so it identifies nobody outside this app — but it is an account identifier,
			  and every follow and size of a signed-in user hangs off it. No email and no name
			  is requested (`requestedScopes = []`).
			-->
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!--
			  A profile's display name ("Anna"), held on the server and printed at the start
			  of that profile's alerts. Free text that is often a real person's first name, so
			  it is declared as Name rather than argued out of it.
			-->
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeName</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!--
			  Credit packs bought and credits spent (`credit_ledger`): the App Store transaction
			  id and pack, each spend's action and time, under the account so a balance survives
			  a reinstall and a refund can be applied. Never a payment detail — Apple takes the
			  payment.
			-->
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurchaseHistory</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!--
			  A garment photographed for the scan, or a scanned garment's own photograph sent to
			  be judged in a fit. Not stored on our server, but Anthropic, which answers it,
			  keeps requests for a limited period, so it is declared as collected. It reaches
			  Anthropic from our server with no device, account or IP address, so not linked.
			-->
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePhotosorVideos</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!--
			  The text of an "Ask Dropwall" search and its replies. Phase 3's plain search is
			  discarded on our server and was not declared; this one is sent to Anthropic, which
			  keeps requests for a limited period. Not linked, for the reason the photographs
			  are not.
			-->
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeSearchHistory</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!--
			  What is sent about the reader's own clothes when they ask: a fit's piece titles and
			  colours, and a daily pick's partner as a short description ("black chore jacket"),
			  whose worded reason is cached on our server for thirty days with no device or
			  account on the row.
			-->
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUserContent</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
